---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods.png";
import catalogueImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/catalogue.png";
import rangeImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/range.png";
import quizImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/quiz.png";
import settingsImage from "../../assets/images/woven-and-woods/theme-settings.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Rebuilding the Woven & Woods website"
	pageTitle="Woven & Woods website case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I rebuilt the Woven & Woods website on a custom WordPress block theme, with around 40 bespoke blocks, structured content and search protected through the move."
	path="/work/woven-and-woods/"
	lede="A custom WordPress build for an independent flooring retailer, designed to make a large catalogue easy to browse, get customers into the showrooms and let staff run the site themselves."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "Woven & Woods, flooring showrooms in Twickenham and Richmond" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Development, content structure, SEO and deployment" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "WordPress block theme, ACF, Claude Code, custom plugins, Rank Math PRO, Cloudflare, CloudPanel" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2026" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The new Woven & Woods homepage"
	link="https://wovenandwoods.com"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			I'd built and run the previous Woven & Woods site, a bespoke WordPress and WooCommerce theme that grew organic search traffic by 95% over three years. But the business had outgrown it, meaning a new solution was required to serve multiple locations and a growing marketing team.
		</p>
		<p>
			The new site had four jobs: make the whole catalogue easy to browse, turn browsing into showroom visits, keep the search rankings the old site had earned and be simple for the less technical members of the marketing team to manage without going through me.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="A catalogue shaped by customers’ needs"
		image={catalogueImage}
		imageAlt="The range catalogue, with category tiles, brand and price filters and range cards"
	>
		<p>
			Most of the site runs on two custom plugins. The first adds around 40 bespoke blocks, created by hand and with AI assistance to meet the very specific needs of the business in ways which had historically been hard to achieve with off-the-shelf plugins. The second handles core features such as branded product URLs, enquiry emails and extending the SEO metadata features of Rank Math.
		</p>
		<p>
			The catalogue lets customers narrow the whole collection by category, brand, price and whether a range is on display in a showroom. I’d spent a lot of time in the showrooms beforehand talking to customers, designers and people in the trades, and these were the first things they generally wanted to know.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Structured content with room to grow"
		image={rangeImage}
		imageAlt="A wood flooring range page showing category, format, showroom availability and size"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			Content is modelled in Advanced Custom Fields as ranges, colour variants, brands, showrooms, case studies and team members, rather than built page by page. Each range page is assembled from that data, so details like format, size and which showroom it's in stay consistent everywhere they appear.
		</p>
		<p>
			The catalogue data was generated using the in-house <a href="/work/product-data-pipeline/">product data pipeline</a> and imported in bulk with WP All Import. Product images are served through Cloudflare's image CDN, using dynamic image transformation to serve the size and quality best suited to the user's device.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Turning undecided browsers into showroom leads"
		image={quizImage}
		imageAlt="The style quiz, asking which room the flooring is for"
	>
		<p>
			Flooring is a big decision and most customers don't know where to start, so the style quiz asks four quick questions, then shows a room the team has installed and the ranges we would recommend to achieve that finish.
		</p>
		<p>
			From there, Fluent Forms and Fluent Booking handle enquiries and showroom appointments, and Google reviews are pulled in through Trustindex.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Built for the marketing team to run without code"
		image={settingsImage}
		imageAlt="The Theme Settings screen in WordPress, with tabs for each section of the site"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			The site's design system (colour roles, type scale and spacing) lives in theme.json, so the WordPress editor uses the brand's styles too and new pages look right by default.
		</p>
		<p>
			Site-wide copy, featured showrooms and featured case studies are edited in a Theme Settings screen powered by ACF, so the marketing team can update the site without a developer.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Maintaining strong search results">
			<p>
				Rank Math PRO handles titles, sitemaps, breadcrumbs and redirects from the old site. Custom structured data marks up showrooms as local businesses, ranges as products, case studies as articles and FAQ sections as FAQs. A dynamically generated llms.txt file gives AI assistants an accurate summary of the business, and analytics run through Google Tag Manager.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="Behind the scenes">
			<p>
				Development happens locally in DDEV, with PHPUnit tests. Code lives in GitHub, and GitHub Actions deploys every change to the staging site automatically. The live site runs on a self-managed VPS with CloudPanel, and I handle all maintenance and updates. Moving off a managed WordPress host gave us more control and simpler deployments.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
